from 2025. We focus\non relevance and on substantive works, many works appear in major conferences and journals such as NeurIPS,\nACL, EMNLP, ICLR, ICML, Science, and Nature. Given the recency, some of the works are unrefereed preprints\nthat are under submission at the time of inclusion. Here we filter for reputable academic and industrial research\nlabs.\n\n1.3   LLM Training Pipeline\nWe provide a brief background of the typical training pipeline of LLMs, introducing relevant terms of the survey.\n   Originally, language models used recurrent architectures such as LSTMs [Hochreiter and Schmidhuber, 1997]\nto embed semantic relations between token structures, allowing limited connections between tokens. The\ntransformer architecture is an effective implementation of the attention mechanism [Vaswani et al., 2017],\nallowing efficient random connections between tokens, improving performance greatly. Encoder transformer\nmodels, such as the BERT family [Devlin et al., 2018], learn embeddings that are suitable for text understanding\nand classification. Decoder transformer models, such as the GPT family [Brown et al., 2020], are trained by\nmasking for text completion and instruction following, and are suitable for text generation.\n\n   Data, Benchmarks, and Performance. LLMs are trained on large datasets [Radford et al., 2019, Wei et al., 2022a].\nPerformance on benchmarks testing formal linguistic competence is high [Warstadt et al., 2019] and so is accuracy\non functional competence or natural language understanding tasks (GLUE, SQUAD, Xsum) [Wang et al., 2018,\n2019, Rajpurkar et al., 2016, Narayan et al., 2018], translation [Kocmi et al., 2022, Papineni et al., 2002, Sennrich\net al., 2015], and question answering [Roberts et al., 2020]. Even in creative domains such as poetry and music\ncomposition LLMs have made some progress [Zhang and Eger, 2024, Yuan et al., 2024b, Xing et al., 2025].\n\n   Models. Popular LLMs are OpenAI’s ChatGPT series [Achiam et al., 2023, Ouyang et al., 2022], Meta’s LLaMa\nfamily [Touvron et al., 2023], Anthropic’s Claude family [Anthropic, 2024], Google’s PaLM [Chowdhery et al.,\n2023] and Gemini [Anil et al., 2023], Qwen [Yang et al., 2025a], and the open-source models BLOOM [Le Scao\net al., 2023], Pythia [Biderman et al., 2023], OLMo Groeneveld et al. [2024], and many others.\n\n                                        Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}, {"page": 6, "text": "1:6   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\n\n\n   Training Pipeline. LLMs are constructed using an elaborate pipeline with different training phases [Radford\net al., 2019, Minaee et al., 2024]. We will briefly describe the phases.\n   1. Acquire a large, general, unlabeled, text corpus [Brown et al., 2020].\n   2. Pretrain a transformer model on the corpus. This step yields a generalist natural language transformer model.\nThe pretraining is done using a self-supervised attention approach [Vaswani et al., 2017] on the unlabeled dataset\n(text corpus).\n   3. Finetune the general model to a specific (narrow) task using a supervised approach on a labeled dataset\nconsisting of prompts and answers (supervised finetuning, SFT) [Wei et al., 2022a, Minaee et al., 2024]. This task\ncan be, for example, translation from one language to another, or questions answering on a certain domain, such\nas medicine.\n   4. Instruction tune for improved instruction following. This is a form of supervised finetuning [Ouyang et al.,\n2022] to improve the ability to answer prompts.\n   5. Align the finetuned model with user expectations (preference alignment). The goal of this step is to improve the\nmodel to give socially acceptable answers such as prevention of hate speech. Popular methods are reinforcement\nlearning with human feedback (RLHF) [Ouyang et al., 2022], direct preference optimization (DPO) [Rafailov et al.,\n2024], or reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) [Lambert et al., 2024].\n   6. Optimize training to improve cost-effectiveness, for example with low-rank optimization (LoRa) [Hu et al.,\n2021], mixed precision training [Micikevicius et al., 2017], or knowledge distillation [Xu et al., 2024b, Gu et al.,\n2023].\n   7. Infer using natural language prompts (instructions). This phase, inference, is the phase where, finally, we\ncan use the fruits of our training efforts. Prompting is the preferred way of using LLMs. In LLMs whose size is\nbeyond hundreds of billions parameters a new learning method emerges: in-context learning [Brown et al., 2020,\nWei et al., 2022a]. This method provides a prompt that contains a small number of examples together with an\ninstruction; it is a form of few-shot learning. However, no parameters of the model are changed by in-context\nlearning, in-context learning takes place at inference time [Dong et al., 2022, Brown et al., 2020].\n   Note that this pipeline is an example of a typical approach. Current pipelines are elaborate, and training is\ncostly. Innovations to training pipelines are the topic of current research, see, for example, Guo et al. [2025], Du\net al. [2025], Lambert et al. [2024].\n\n1.4       The Need for Agentic LLMs\nWhile the performance of LLMs continues to amaze in many domains, four challenges have emerged in the recent\nliterature.\n   1. Prompt engineering Originally LLMs were trained as straight decoders, to be used with instruction prompts.\nThe prompts contain context and instructions, and the model replies. The user interacts directly with the model,\nand writes the prompts themselves. LLMs turned out to be quite sensitive to small differences in the prompt\nformulation. When an answer is not satisfactory, the user has to remember the history of the interaction, and\nhas to improve the prompt. This is known as prompt engineering. With basic LLMs, prompt improvement is a\ntedious, manual, task.\n   2. Hallucination When LLMs provide answers that look good, but are factually incorrect, they are said to\nhallucinate. Hallucination is a major problem of LLMs. It is caused, in part, by a lack of grounding. LLMs are trained\nto predict one of the statistically most probable next tokens, based on the training corpus. Since models are aligned\nto human preferences during fine-tuning, they often provide answers that look good by these standards while\nnot adhering to other criteria, such as factuality. Various methods have been developed to mitigate hallucination,\nsuch as detecting uncertainty through self-reflection on their own answers, and with mechanistic interpretability\nmethods [Conmy et al., 2023]. We will review papers that discuss these method in this survey.\n\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}, {"page": 7, "text": "                                                                                                                  Agentic LLM     •   1:7\n\n\n                                                                                2.1 Multi-step\n\n                                               2. Reasoning                  2.2 Self Reflection\n\n                                                                       2.3 Retrieval Augmentation\n\n                                                                          3.1 World Models/VLA\n\n                         Agentic LLM             3. Acting                     3.2 Robot/Tools\n\n                                                                                3.3 Assistants\n\n                                                                           4.1 Social Capabilities\n\n                                              4. Interacting            4.2 Role-based Interaction\n\n                                                                        4.3 Open-ended Societies\n\n      Fig. 3. Agentic LLM Taxonomy of Reasoning, Acting, Interacting, with their sub-categories (see the Subsections)\n\n\n   3. Reasoning Another well-reported challenge for LLMs is (mathematical) reasoning [Cobbe et al., 2021, Plaat\net al., 2025]. LLMs used to be quite bad at solving math word problems (such as: “Annie has a one pie that she cuts\ninto twelve pieces. She eats one third of the pieces. How many pieces does she have left?”). Reasoning challenges\nhave given rise to step-by-step problem solving methods, such as reported by Wei et al. [2022b], both implicit,\nand with explicit (neurosymbolic) prompt optimization methods [Yao et al., 2024]. This too we discuss in the next\nsection.\n   4. Training Data LLMs are as smart as the data allows that was available at training time. When datasets no\nlonger improve, pretraining and finetuning can no longer improve language models, and other learning methods\nare needed [Sutskever, 2024]. Any event that happened after training, or any information available in special\ndatabases, are not in the model [Lewis et al., 2020].\n   These four challenges have led to the introduction of inference-time in-context learning, retrieval, and inter-\naction methods. The methods involve automated prompt-improvement, retrieval of extra data, usage of tools,\ninteraction with other LLMs, self-verification, and simulations. As we will see in this survey, these works have\nyielded more intelligent, active, and interactive LLMs— agentic LLMs.\n\n1.5    Taxonomy\nIn a short amount of time, a literature on agentic LLMs has appeared, that we categorize based on the above\nchallenges. The agentic LLMs in this survey have (1) reasoning capabilities, (2) an interface to the outside world\nin order to act, and (3) a social environment with other agents with which to interact. The agentic LLMs in some\nof the discussed works have all three elements. We also review papers concerning LLMs that do not have all\nthree elements, in order to include relevant technologies and applications. A picture of the taxonomy is shown in\nFigure 3. The subcategories are explained below.\n   As we noted before, the three categories in our taxonomy come from three different backgrounds. To be\nintelligent, LLMs are enhanced with reasoning, combining deep learning with the symbolic AI tradition [Yu\net al., 2024a, Li et al., 2025b]. To be active, LLMs are enhanced with tools that can act in the world (including\nrobots, that plan to move in the world). To be social, LLMs are placed in interactive settings with other agents.\nThey rely partially on capacities already present in traditional LLMs, such as basic theory of mind abilities and\n\n                                         Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}, {"page": 8, "text": "1:8   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\n\n\nunderstanding of game theory and social dilemmas. Agentic LLMs learn to interact better by adapting their\nintelligence.\n   We use this taxonomy in the remainder of the survey to organize the agentic LLM literature, see Figure 3.\nThe three main categories can be found in Section 2, 3, and 4. The subtopics are described in the corresponding\nSubsections.\n   Reasoning (Table 1). Earlier progress in multi-step reasoning LLMs and retrieval augmentation has enabled\nmuch of the current developments in agentic LLMs (category 1). In this category, the aim is to address challenges\nin solving math word problems, and in providing up to date answers to queries. The contributions to intelligent\nLLMs came out of the need to improve multi-step reasoning of basic LLMs (sub-category a). In reasoning LLMs,\nmethods from planning and search are used to let the LLM follow a step-by-step reasoning path. More elaborate\nsearch algorithms allow automated prompt-improvement and self reflection (sub-category b). Finally, certain\nquestions can only be answered by inference-time data retrieval (sub-category c). The focus is on the individual\nimprovement of the intelligent LLM agent.\n   Acting (Table 2). In category 2, acting, the aim is to perform actions in the world, to assist the user, as shown\nin Figure 2. Sub-category a discusses world-models and multi-modal vision-language-action models. These are\nmodels for robots to learn which actions to take to achieve a task in a certain visual setting. In sub-category b, we\nreview how tools can be used by LLMs through an application programming interface (API), and how robots can\nplan actions. Sub-category c discusses how these tools can be used as assistants of users, to perform tasks such as\nmaking travel arrangements, performing medical suggestions, or giving trading advice.\n   Interacting (Table 3). Category 3 is about interaction in multi-agent simulations. Here, in sub-category a, we first\nstudy basic social capabilities of LLMs on which interactions can build. Second, in sub-category b, we study how\nLLM agents can work together using simulations where they are assigned specific roles. Third, in sub-category\nc, we study emergence of collective phenomena in open-ended interactions, such as social coordination via\nconventions and norms. Here the focus is on the emergent interaction level of the agent society. Multi-agent\nsimulation with LLMs is becoming an active field for studying questions from the social sciences that previous\ngenerations of agent-based models were unable to address.\n   Taxonomy. A picture of the taxonomy is shown in Figure 3. In addition, the surveyed papers are listed in three\ntables, Tables 1–3. The tables show the name of the approach, the type of reasoning that they use (category 1),\nthe application area in which they assist (category 2), and the type of social interaction that they have (category\n3). Most approaches focus on one of these aspects, and their main category is shown in the table.\n\n2     Reasoning\nWe will now turn to the first category, reasoning. We discuss reasoning-related inference-time improvements to\nLLMs, to improve decision making. Intelligent decision making can be achieved by retrieving more and better\ninformation, and by improving LLM performance on reasoning problems. First we review methods that prompt\nan LLM to take a step-by-step approach in solving these problems. Next, we review methods that improve these\nprompts through self reflection. Finally, we review retrieval augmentation methods.\n   Note that both retrieval augmentation and self reflection can be used to generate new training data. Retrieval\naugmentation can be used to retrieve relevant information beyond the originally available training dataset. Self\nreflection uses methods related to planning that imagine plausible futures, that can be useful for training of\nLLMs. Originally, the methods that we review in this section were developed with the goal of improving the\npredictive modeling performance of the LLM. For the field of agentic LLMs, the reasoning techniques are used as\nan important fundament for agents that act with the world, and interact with each other.\n\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}], "evidence_ids": ["ev-000012", "ev-000013", "ev-000014", "ev-000015"]}}
{"event": "code_execution", "time": 1793187692.2956903, "run_id": "c91ee58aa1e049578717e6c44905d773", "seq": 110, "step_id": 10, "stdout": "", "output_truncated": false, "error": null, "tool_calls": [{"name": "update_plan", "args": {"items": [{"title": "检查工作副本、测试与报告初始结构", "status": "done"}, {"title": "分批读取 PDF 第1-52页并记录五主题笔记", "status": "doing"}, {"title": "核对主题问题、引文与页码", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "归并五份笔记并填写 report.json", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "运行 pytest 并完整验证报告", "status": "todo"}]}, "result": {"items": [{"title": "检查工作副本、测试与报告初始结构", "status": "done"}, {"title": "分批读取 PDF 第1-52页并记录五主题笔记", "status": "doing"}, {"title": "核对主题问题、引文与页码", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "归并五份笔记并填写 report.json", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "运行 pytest 并完整验证报告", "status": "todo"}]}}, {"name": "read_pdf", "args": {"start_page": 5, "end_page": 8}, "result": {"argv": ["/mnt/d/Muiti-Agent Programing/agents/code-agent/24371220/.venv/bin/python", "read_pdf.py", "--start-page", "5", "--end-page", "8"], "cwd": "/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/run-nh47wiy_/results/paper/repo", "rc": 0, "stdout": "{\"pdf\": \"/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/run-nh47wiy_/results/paper/repo/paper.pdf\", \"pages\": [{\"page\": 5, \"text\": \"                                                                                                                 Agentic LLM     •   1:5\\n\\n\\net al., 2025, Lambert et al., 2024]. The categories also correspond to fields in artificial intelligence that have a long\\nresearch tradition across symbolic AI, robotics/autonomous systems, and connectionism/multi-agent modeling,\\nrespectively. Agentic LLMs are thus both a recent development and build on decades of research. This is reflected\\nin our discussion below.\\n\\n1.2   Literature Selection\\nThe field of agentic LLM is rich and active. This survey can only cover the current status of the field. We\\nhope to provide clarity about the main approaches, to ease the entry of new researchers into the field. The\\npapers were initially selected with a Google Scholar search on Agentic LLM. From there, we used a snowballing\\napproach to discover work that was cited but not yet included in our initial set. We have only selected LLM-based\\napproaches, excluding multi-agent work without LLMs. In addition, some works on LLMs that do not involve\\nagentic augmentations are included to provide background.\\n   Related surveys on agents and LLMs are starting to appear. Li [2024] reviews retrieval and tool use in agentic\\nLLMs. Wang et al. [2024b] focuses on autonomy and agent construction. Gao et al. [2024] also provide an extensive\\noverview, and focus on multi-agent modeling and simulation. Xi et al. [2023] again focus on the construction of\\ninteractive agents, using a more explanatory anthropomorphic approach of perception, brain, and action. An\\nextensive general survey of LLMs is Zhao et al. [2023], a slightly smaller one is [Minaee et al., 2024], an earlier\\nsurvey is [Min et al., 2023]. Yin et al. [2023] review works on multimodal LLMs.\\n   We focus on recent work; most of the works are from 2024, some are from 2023, and some from 2025. We focus\\non relevance and on substantive works, many works appear in major conferences and journals such as NeurIPS,\\nACL, EMNLP, ICLR, ICML, Science, and Nature. Given the recency, some of the works are unrefereed preprints\\nthat are under submission at the time of inclusion. Here we filter for reputable academic and industrial research\\nlabs.\\n\\n1.3   LLM Training Pipeline\\nWe provide a brief background of the typical training pipeline of LLMs, introducing relevant terms of the survey.\\n   Originally, language models used recurrent architectures such as LSTMs [Hochreiter and Schmidhuber, 1997]\\nto embed semantic relations between token structures, allowing limited connections between tokens. The\\ntransformer architecture is an effective implementation of the attention mechanism [Vaswani et al., 2017],\\nallowing efficient random connections between tokens, improving performance greatly. Encoder transformer\\nmodels, such as the BERT family [Devlin et al., 2018], learn embeddings that are suitable for text understanding\\nand classification. Decoder transformer models, such as the GPT family [Brown et al., 2020], are trained by\\nmasking for text completion and instruction following, and are suitable for text generation.\\n\\n   Data, Benchmarks, and Performance. LLMs are trained on large datasets [Radford et al., 2019, Wei et al., 2022a].\\nPerformance on benchmarks testing formal linguistic competence is high [Warstadt et al., 2019] and so is accuracy\\non functional competence or natural language understanding tasks (GLUE, SQUAD, Xsum) [Wang et al., 2018,\\n2019, Rajpurkar et al., 2016, Narayan et al., 2018], translation [Kocmi et al., 2022, Papineni et al., 2002, Sennrich\\net al., 2015], and question answering [Roberts et al., 2020]. Even in creative domains such as poetry and music\\ncomposition LLMs have made some progress [Zhang and Eger, 2024, Yuan et al., 2024b, Xing et al., 2025].\\n\\n   Models. Popular LLMs are OpenAI’s ChatGPT series [Achiam et al., 2023, Ouyang et al., 2022], Meta’s LLaMa\\nfamily [Touvron et al., 2023], Anthropic’s Claude family [Anthropic, 2024], Google’s PaLM [Chowdhery et al.,\\n2023] and Gemini [Anil et al., 2023], Qwen [Yang et al., 2025a], and the open-source models BLOOM [Le Scao\\net al., 2023], Pythia [Biderman et al., 2023], OLMo Groeneveld et al. [2024], and many others.\\n\\n                                        Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}, {\"page\": 6, \"text\": \"1:6   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\\n\\n\\n   Training Pipeline. LLMs are constructed using an elaborate pipeline with different training phases [Radford\\net al., 2019, Minaee et al., 2024]. We will briefly describe the phases.\\n   1. Acquire a large, general, unlabeled, text corpus [Brown et al., 2020].\\n   2. Pretrain a transformer model on the corpus. This step yields a generalist natural language transformer model.\\nThe pretraining is done using a self-supervised attention approach [Vaswani et al., 2017] on the unlabeled dataset\\n(text corpus).\\n   3. Finetune the general model to a specific (narrow) task using a supervised approach on a labeled dataset\\nconsisting of prompts and answers (supervised finetuning, SFT) [Wei et al., 2022a, Minaee et al., 2024]. This task\\ncan be, for example, translation from one language to another, or questions answering on a certain domain, such\\nas medicine.\\n   4. Instruction tune for improved instruction following. This is a form of supervised finetuning [Ouyang et al.,\\n2022] to improve the ability to answer prompts.\\n   5. Align the finetuned model with user expectations (preference alignment). The goal of this step is to improve the\\nmodel to give socially acceptable answers such as prevention of hate speech. Popular methods are reinforcement\\nlearning with human feedback (RLHF) [Ouyang et al., 2022], direct preference optimization (DPO) [Rafailov et al.,\\n2024], or reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) [Lambert et al., 2024].\\n   6. Optimize training to improve cost-effectiveness, for example with low-rank optimization (LoRa) [Hu et al.,\\n2021], mixed precision training [Micikevicius et al., 2017], or knowledge distillation [Xu et al., 2024b, Gu et al.,\\n2023].\\n   7. Infer using natural language prompts (instructions). This phase, inference, is the phase where, finally, we\\ncan use the fruits of our training efforts. Prompting is the preferred way of using LLMs. In LLMs whose size is\\nbeyond hundreds of billions parameters a new learning method emerges: in-context learning [Brown et al., 2020,\\nWei et al., 2022a]. This method provides a prompt that contains a small number of examples together with an\\ninstruction; it is a form of few-shot learning. However, no parameters of the model are changed by in-context\\nlearning, in-context learning takes place at inference time [Dong et al., 2022, Brown et al., 2020].\\n   Note that this pipeline is an example of a typical approach. Current pipelines are elaborate, and training is\\ncostly. Innovations to training pipelines are the topic of current research, see, for example, Guo et al. [2025], Du\\net al. [2025], Lambert et al. [2024].\\n\\n1.4       The Need for Agentic LLMs\\nWhile the performance of LLMs continues to amaze in many domains, four challenges have emerged in the recent\\nliterature.\\n   1. Prompt engineering Originally LLMs were trained as straight decoders, to be used with instruction prompts.\\nThe prompts contain context and instructions, and the model replies. The user interacts directly with the model,\\nand writes the prompts themselves. LLMs turned out to be quite sensitive to small differences in the prompt\\nformulation. When an answer is not satisfactory, the user has to remember the history of the interaction, and\\nhas to improve the prompt. This is known as prompt engineering. With basic LLMs, prompt improvement is a\\ntedious, manual, task.\\n   2. Hallucination When LLMs provide answers that look good, but are factually incorrect, they are said to\\nhallucinate. Hallucination is a major problem of LLMs. It is caused, in part, by a lack of grounding. LLMs are trained\\nto predict one of the statistically most probable next tokens, based on the training corpus. Since models are aligned\\nto human preferences during fine-tuning, they often provide answers that look good by these standards while\\nnot adhering to other criteria, such as factuality. Various methods have been developed to mitigate hallucination,\\nsuch as detecting uncertainty through self-reflection on their own answers, and with mechanistic interpretability\\nmethods [Conmy et al., 2023]. We will review papers that discuss these method in this survey.\\n\\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}, {\"page\": 7, \"text\": \"                                                                                                                  Agentic LLM     •   1:7\\n\\n\\n                                                                                2.1 Multi-step\\n\\n                                               2. Reasoning                  2.2 Self Reflection\\n\\n                                                                       2.3 Retrieval Augmentation\\n\\n                                                                          3.1 World Models/VLA\\n\\n                         Agentic LLM             3. Acting                     3.2 Robot/Tools\\n\\n                                                                                3.3 Assistants\\n\\n                                                                           4.1 Social Capabilities\\n\\n                                              4. Interacting            4.2 Role-based Interaction\\n\\n                                                                        4.3 Open-ended Societies\\n\\n      Fig. 3. Agentic LLM Taxonomy of Reasoning, Acting, Interacting, with their sub-categories (see the Subsections)\\n\\n\\n   3. Reasoning Another well-reported challenge for LLMs is (mathematical) reasoning [Cobbe et al., 2021, Plaat\\net al., 2025]. LLMs used to be quite bad at solving math word problems (such as: “Annie has a one pie that she cuts\\ninto twelve pieces. She eats one third of the pieces. How many pieces does she have left?”). Reasoning challenges\\nhave given rise to step-by-step problem solving methods, such as reported by Wei et al. [2022b], both implicit,\\nand with explicit (neurosymbolic) prompt optimization methods [Yao et al., 2024]. This too we discuss in the next\\nsection.\\n   4. Training Data LLMs are as smart as the data allows that was available at training time. When datasets no\\nlonger improve, pretraining and finetuning can no longer improve language models, and other learning methods\\nare needed [Sutskever, 2024]. Any event that happened after training, or any information available in special\\ndatabases, are not in the model [Lewis et al., 2020].\\n   These four challenges have led to the introduction of inference-time in-context learning, retrieval, and inter-\\naction methods. The methods involve automated prompt-improvement, retrieval of extra data, usage of tools,\\ninteraction with other LLMs, self-verification, and simulations. As we will see in this survey, these works have\\nyielded more intelligent, active, and interactive LLMs— agentic LLMs.\\n\\n1.5    Taxonomy\\nIn a short amount of time, a literature on agentic LLMs has appeared, that we categorize based on the above\\nchallenges. The agentic LLMs in this survey have (1) reasoning capabilities, (2) an interface to the outside world\\nin order to act, and (3) a social environment with other agents with which to interact. The agentic LLMs in some\\nof the discussed works have all three elements. We also review papers concerning LLMs that do not have all\\nthree elements, in order to include relevant technologies and applications. A picture of the taxonomy is shown in\\nFigure 3. The subcategories are explained below.\\n   As we noted before, the three categories in our taxonomy come from three different backgrounds. To be\\nintelligent, LLMs are enhanced with reasoning, combining deep learning with the symbolic AI tradition [Yu\\net al., 2024a, Li et al., 2025b]. To be active, LLMs are enhanced with tools that can act in the world (including\\nrobots, that plan to move in the world). To be social, LLMs are placed in interactive settings with other agents.\\nThey rely partially on capacities already present in traditional LLMs, such as basic theory of mind abilities and\\n\\n                                         Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}, {\"page\": 8, \"text\": \"1:8   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\\n\\n\\nunderstanding of game theory and social dilemmas. Agentic LLMs learn to interact better by adapting their\\nintelligence.\\n   We use this taxonomy in the remainder of the survey to organize the agentic LLM literature, see Figure 3.\\nThe three main categories can be found in Section 2, 3, and 4. The subtopics are described in the corresponding\\nSubsections.\\n   Reasoning (Table 1). Earlier progress in multi-step reasoning LLMs and retrieval augmentation has enabled\\nmuch of the current developments in agentic LLMs (category 1). In this category, the aim is to address challenges\\nin solving math word problems, and in providing up to date answers to queries. The contributions to intelligent\\nLLMs came out of the need to improve multi-step reasoning of basic LLMs (sub-category a). In reasoning LLMs,\\nmethods from planning and search are used to let the LLM follow a step-by-step reasoning path. More elaborate\\nsearch algorithms allow automated prompt-improvement and self reflection (sub-category b). Finally, certain\\nquestions can only be answered by inference-time data retrieval (sub-category c). The focus is on the individual\\nimprovement of the intelligent LLM agent.\\n   Acting (Table 2). In category 2, acting, the aim is to perform actions in the world, to assist the user, as shown\\nin Figure 2. Sub-category a discusses world-models and multi-modal vision-language-action models. These are\\nmodels for robots to learn which actions to take to achieve a task in a certain visual setting. In sub-category b, we\\nreview how tools can be used by LLMs through an application programming interface (API), and how robots can\\nplan actions. Sub-category c discusses how these tools can be used as assistants of users, to perform tasks such as\\nmaking travel arrangements, performing medical suggestions, or giving trading advice.\\n   Interacting (Table 3). Category 3 is about interaction in multi-agent simulations. Here, in sub-category a, we first\\nstudy basic social capabilities of LLMs on which interactions can build. Second, in sub-category b, we study how\\nLLM agents can work together using simulations where they are assigned specific roles. Third, in sub-category\\nc, we study emergence of collective phenomena in open-ended interactions, such as social coordination via\\nconventions and norms. Here the focus is on the emergent interaction level of the agent society. Multi-agent\\nsimulation with LLMs is becoming an active field for studying questions from the social sciences that previous\\ngenerations of agent-based models were unable to address.\\n   Taxonomy. A picture of the taxonomy is shown in Figure 3. In addition, the surveyed papers are listed in three\\ntables, Tables 1–3. The tables show the name of the approach, the type of reasoning that they use (category 1),\\nthe application area in which they assist (category 2), and the type of social interaction that they have (category\\n3). Most approaches focus on one of these aspects, and their main category is shown in the table.\\n\\n2     Reasoning\\nWe will now turn to the first category, reasoning. We discuss reasoning-related inference-time improvements to\\nLLMs, to improve decision making. Intelligent decision making can be achieved by retrieving more and better\\ninformation, and by improving LLM performance on reasoning problems. First we review methods that prompt\\nan LLM to take a step-by-step approach in solving these problems. Next, we review methods that improve these\\nprompts through self reflection. Finally, we review retrieval augmentation methods.\\n   Note that both retrieval augmentation and self reflection can be used to generate new training data. Retrieval\\naugmentation can be used to retrieve relevant information beyond the originally available training dataset. Self\\nreflection uses methods related to planning that imagine plausible futures, that can be useful for training of\\nLLMs. Originally, the methods that we review in this section were developed with the goal of improving the\\npredictive modeling performance of the LLM. For the field of agentic LLMs, the reasoning techniques are used as\\nan important fundament for agents that act with the world, and interact with each other.\\n\\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}]}\n", "stderr": "", "timed_out": false, "pdf": "/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/run-nh47wiy_/results/paper/repo/paper.pdf", "pages": [{"page": 5, "text": "                                                                                                                 Agentic LLM     •   1:5\n\n\net al., 2025, Lambert et al., 2024]. The categories also correspond to fields in artificial intelligence that have a long\nresearch tradition across symbolic AI, robotics/autonomous systems, and connectionism/multi-agent modeling,\nrespectively. Agentic LLMs are thus both a recent development and build on decades of research. This is reflected\nin our discussion below.\n\n1.2   Literature Selection\nThe field of agentic LLM is rich and active. This survey can only cover the current status of the field. We\nhope to provide clarity about the main approaches, to ease the entry of new researchers into the field. The\npapers were initially selected with a Google Scholar search on Agentic LLM. From there, we used a snowballing\napproach to discover work that was cited but not yet included in our initial set. We have only selected LLM-based\napproaches, excluding multi-agent work without LLMs. In addition, some works on LLMs that do not involve\nagentic augmentations are included to provide background.\n   Related surveys on agents and LLMs are starting to appear. Li [2024] reviews retrieval and tool use in agentic\nLLMs. Wang et al. [2024b] focuses on autonomy and agent construction. Gao et al. [2024] also provide an extensive\noverview, and focus on multi-agent modeling and simulation. Xi et al. [2023] again focus on the construction of\ninteractive agents, using a more explanatory anthropomorphic approach of perception, brain, and action. An\nextensive general survey of LLMs is Zhao et al. [2023], a slightly smaller one is [Minaee et al., 2024], an earlier\nsurvey is [Min et al., 2023]. Yin et al. [2023] review works on multimodal LLMs.\n   We focus on recent work; most of the works are from 2024, some are from 2023, and some from 2025. We focus\non relevance and on substantive works, many works appear in major conferences and journals such as NeurIPS,\nACL, EMNLP, ICLR, ICML, Science, and Nature. Given the recency, some of the works are unrefereed preprints\nthat are under submission at the time of inclusion. Here we filter for reputable academic and industrial research\nlabs.\n\n1.3   LLM Training Pipeline\nWe provide a brief background of the typical training pipeline of LLMs, introducing relevant terms of the survey.\n   Originally, language models used recurrent architectures such as LSTMs [Hochreiter and Schmidhuber, 1997]\nto embed semantic relations between token structures, allowing limited connections between tokens. The\ntransformer architecture is an effective implementation of the attention mechanism [Vaswani et al., 2017],\nallowing efficient random connections between tokens, improving performance greatly. Encoder transformer\nmodels, such as the BERT family [Devlin et al., 2018], learn embeddings that are suitable for text understanding\nand classification. Decoder transformer models, such as the GPT family [Brown et al., 2020], are trained by\nmasking for text completion and instruction following, and are suitable for text generation.\n\n   Data, Benchmarks, and Performance. LLMs are trained on large datasets [Radford et al., 2019, Wei et al., 2022a].\nPerformance on benchmarks testing formal linguistic competence is high [Warstadt et al., 2019] and so is accuracy\non functional competence or natural language understanding tasks (GLUE, SQUAD, Xsum) [Wang et al., 2018,\n2019, Rajpurkar et al., 2016, Narayan et al., 2018], translation [Kocmi et al., 2022, Papineni et al., 2002, Sennrich\net al., 2015], and question answering [Roberts et al., 2020]. Even in creative domains such as poetry and music\ncomposition LLMs have made some progress [Zhang and Eger, 2024, Yuan et al., 2024b, Xing et al., 2025].\n\n   Models. Popular LLMs are OpenAI’s ChatGPT series [Achiam et al., 2023, Ouyang et al., 2022], Meta’s LLaMa\nfamily [Touvron et al., 2023], Anthropic’s Claude family [Anthropic, 2024], Google’s PaLM [Chowdhery et al.,\n2023] and Gemini [Anil et al., 2023], Qwen [Yang et al., 2025a], and the open-source models BLOOM [Le Scao\net al., 2023], Pythia [Biderman et al., 2023], OLMo Groeneveld et al. [2024], and many others.\n\n                                        Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}, {"page": 6, "text": "1:6   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\n\n\n   Training Pipeline. LLMs are constructed using an elaborate pipeline with different training phases [Radford\net al., 2019, Minaee et al., 2024]. We will briefly describe the phases.\n   1. Acquire a large, general, unlabeled, text corpus [Brown et al., 2020].\n   2. Pretrain a transformer model on the corpus. This step yields a generalist natural language transformer model.\nThe pretraining is done using a self-supervised attention approach [Vaswani et al., 2017] on the unlabeled dataset\n(text corpus).\n   3. Finetune the general model to a specific (narrow) task using a supervised approach on a labeled dataset\nconsisting of prompts and answers (supervised finetuning, SFT) [Wei et al., 2022a, Minaee et al., 2024]. This task\ncan be, for example, translation from one language to another, or questions answering on a certain domain, such\nas medicine.\n   4. Instruction tune for improved instruction following. This is a form of supervised finetuning [Ouyang et al.,\n2022] to improve the ability to answer prompts.\n   5. Align the finetuned model with user expectations (preference alignment). The goal of this step is to improve the\nmodel to give socially acceptable answers such as prevention of hate speech. Popular methods are reinforcement\nlearning with human feedback (RLHF) [Ouyang et al., 2022], direct preference optimization (DPO) [Rafailov et al.,\n2024], or reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) [Lambert et al., 2024].\n   6. Optimize training to improve cost-effectiveness, for example with low-rank optimization (LoRa) [Hu et al.,\n2021], mixed precision training [Micikevicius et al., 2017], or knowledge distillation [Xu et al., 2024b, Gu et al.,\n2023].\n   7. Infer using natural language prompts (instructions). This phase, inference, is the phase where, finally, we\ncan use the fruits of our training efforts. Prompting is the preferred way of using LLMs. In LLMs whose size is\nbeyond hundreds of billions parameters a new learning method emerges: in-context learning [Brown et al., 2020,\nWei et al., 2022a]. This method provides a prompt that contains a small number of examples together with an\ninstruction; it is a form of few-shot learning. However, no parameters of the model are changed by in-context\nlearning, in-context learning takes place at inference time [Dong et al., 2022, Brown et al., 2020].\n   Note that this pipeline is an example of a typical approach. Current pipelines are elaborate, and training is\ncostly. Innovations to training pipelines are the topic of current research, see, for example, Guo et al. [2025], Du\net al. [2025], Lambert et al. [2024].\n\n1.4       The Need for Agentic LLMs\nWhile the performance of LLMs continues to amaze in many domains, four challenges have emerged in the recent\nliterature.\n   1. Prompt engineering Originally LLMs were trained as straight decoders, to be used with instruction prompts.\nThe prompts contain context and instructions, and the model replies. The user interacts directly with the model,\nand writes the prompts themselves. LLMs turned out to be quite sensitive to small differences in the prompt\nformulation. When an answer is not satisfactory, the user has to remember the history of the interaction, and\nhas to improve the prompt. This is known as prompt engineering. With basic LLMs, prompt improvement is a\ntedious, manual, task.\n   2. Hallucination When LLMs provide answers that look good, but are factually incorrect, they are said to\nhallucinate. Hallucination is a major problem of LLMs. It is caused, in part, by a lack of grounding. LLMs are trained\nto predict one of the statistically most probable next tokens, based on the training corpus. Since models are aligned\nto human preferences during fine-tuning, they often provide answers that look good by these standards while\nnot adhering to other criteria, such as factuality. Various methods have been developed to mitigate hallucination,\nsuch as detecting uncertainty through self-reflection on their own answers, and with mechanistic interpretability\nmethods [Conmy et al., 2023]. We will review papers that discuss these method in this survey.\n\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}, {"page": 7, "text": "                                                                                                                  Agentic LLM     •   1:7\n\n\n                                                                                2.1 Multi-step\n\n                                               2. Reasoning                  2.2 Self Reflection\n\n                                                                       2.3 Retrieval Augmentation\n\n                                                                          3.1 World Models/VLA\n\n                         Agentic LLM             3. Acting                     3.2 Robot/Tools\n\n                                                                                3.3 Assistants\n\n                                                                           4.1 Social Capabilities\n\n                                              4. Interacting            4.2 Role-based Interaction\n\n                                                                        4.3 Open-ended Societies\n\n      Fig. 3. Agentic LLM Taxonomy of Reasoning, Acting, Interacting, with their sub-categories (see the Subsections)\n\n\n   3. Reasoning Another well-reported challenge for LLMs is (mathematical) reasoning [Cobbe et al., 2021, Plaat\net al., 2025]. LLMs used to be quite bad at solving math word problems (such as: “Annie has a one pie that she cuts\ninto twelve pieces. She eats one third of the pieces. How many pieces does she have left?”). Reasoning challenges\nhave given rise to step-by-step problem solving methods, such as reported by Wei et al. [2022b], both implicit,\nand with explicit (neurosymbolic) prompt optimization methods [Yao et al., 2024]. This too we discuss in the next\nsection.\n   4. Training Data LLMs are as smart as the data allows that was available at training time. When datasets no\nlonger improve, pretraining and finetuning can no longer improve language models, and other learning methods\nare needed [Sutskever, 2024]. Any event that happened after training, or any information available in special\ndatabases, are not in the model [Lewis et al., 2020].\n   These four challenges have led to the introduction of inference-time in-context learning, retrieval, and inter-\naction methods. The methods involve automated prompt-improvement, retrieval of extra data, usage of tools,\ninteraction with other LLMs, self-verification, and simulations. As we will see in this survey, these works have\nyielded more intelligent, active, and interactive LLMs— agentic LLMs.\n\n1.5    Taxonomy\nIn a short amount of time, a literature on agentic LLMs has appeared, that we categorize based on the above\nchallenges. The agentic LLMs in this survey have (1) reasoning capabilities, (2) an interface to the outside world\nin order to act, and (3) a social environment with other agents with which to interact. The agentic LLMs in some\nof the discussed works have all three elements. We also review papers concerning LLMs that do not have all\nthree elements, in order to include relevant technologies and applications. A picture of the taxonomy is shown in\nFigure 3. The subcategories are explained below.\n   As we noted before, the three categories in our taxonomy come from three different backgrounds. To be\nintelligent, LLMs are enhanced with reasoning, combining deep learning with the symbolic AI tradition [Yu\net al., 2024a, Li et al., 2025b]. To be active, LLMs are enhanced with tools that can act in the world (including\nrobots, that plan to move in the world). To be social, LLMs are placed in interactive settings with other agents.\nThey rely partially on capacities already present in traditional LLMs, such as basic theory of mind abilities and\n\n                                         Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}, {"page": 8, "text": "1:8   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\n\n\nunderstanding of game theory and social dilemmas. Agentic LLMs learn to interact better by adapting their\nintelligence.\n   We use this taxonomy in the remainder of the survey to organize the agentic LLM literature, see Figure 3.\nThe three main categories can be found in Section 2, 3, and 4. The subtopics are described in the corresponding\nSubsections.\n   Reasoning (Table 1). Earlier progress in multi-step reasoning LLMs and retrieval augmentation has enabled\nmuch of the current developments in agentic LLMs (category 1). In this category, the aim is to address challenges\nin solving math word problems, and in providing up to date answers to queries. The contributions to intelligent\nLLMs came out of the need to improve multi-step reasoning of basic LLMs (sub-category a). In reasoning LLMs,\nmethods from planning and search are used to let the LLM follow a step-by-step reasoning path. More elaborate\nsearch algorithms allow automated prompt-improvement and self reflection (sub-category b). Finally, certain\nquestions can only be answered by inference-time data retrieval (sub-category c). The focus is on the individual\nimprovement of the intelligent LLM agent.\n   Acting (Table 2). In category 2, acting, the aim is to perform actions in the world, to assist the user, as shown\nin Figure 2. Sub-category a discusses world-models and multi-modal vision-language-action models. These are\nmodels for robots to learn which actions to take to achieve a task in a certain visual setting. In sub-category b, we\nreview how tools can be used by LLMs through an application programming interface (API), and how robots can\nplan actions. Sub-category c discusses how these tools can be used as assistants of users, to perform tasks such as\nmaking travel arrangements, performing medical suggestions, or giving trading advice.\n   Interacting (Table 3). Category 3 is about interaction in multi-agent simulations. Here, in sub-category a, we first\nstudy basic social capabilities of LLMs on which interactions can build. Second, in sub-category b, we study how\nLLM agents can work together using simulations where they are assigned specific roles. Third, in sub-category\nc, we study emergence of collective phenomena in open-ended interactions, such as social coordination via\nconventions and norms. Here the focus is on the emergent interaction level of the agent society. Multi-agent\nsimulation with LLMs is becoming an active field for studying questions from the social sciences that previous\ngenerations of agent-based models were unable to address.\n   Taxonomy. A picture of the taxonomy is shown in Figure 3. In addition, the surveyed papers are listed in three\ntables, Tables 1–3. The tables show the name of the approach, the type of reasoning that they use (category 1),\nthe application area in which they assist (category 2), and the type of social interaction that they have (category\n3). Most approaches focus on one of these aspects, and their main category is shown in the table.\n\n2     Reasoning\nWe will now turn to the first category, reasoning. We discuss reasoning-related inference-time improvements to\nLLMs, to improve decision making. Intelligent decision making can be achieved by retrieving more and better\ninformation, and by improving LLM performance on reasoning problems. First we review methods that prompt\nan LLM to take a step-by-step approach in solving these problems. Next, we review methods that improve these\nprompts through self reflection. Finally, we review retrieval augmentation methods.\n   Note that both retrieval augmentation and self reflection can be used to generate new training data. Retrieval\naugmentation can be used to retrieve relevant information beyond the originally available training dataset. Self\nreflection uses methods related to planning that imagine plausible futures, that can be useful for training of\nLLMs. Originally, the methods that we review in this section were developed with the goal of improving the\npredictive modeling performance of the LLM. For the field of agentic LLMs, the reasoning techniques are used as\nan important fundament for agents that act with the world, and interact with each other.\n\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}], "evidence_ids": ["ev-000012", "ev-000013", "ev-000014", "ev-000015"]}}], "namespace": [], "sources": {}}
