{"event": "tool_result", "time": 1793187704.490196, "run_id": "c91ee58aa1e049578717e6c44905d773", "seq": 132, "step_id": 12, "tool_call_id": "tool-00022", "action": {"tool": "get_evidence", "evidence_id": "ev-000011"}, "result": {"id": "ev-000011", "content": "1:4   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\n\n\n\n\n                                      Fig. 2. LLM Agent as Assistant [Sypherd and Belle, 2024].\n\n\n\n   Agency is a central concept in artificial intelligence [Russell and Norvig, 2016]. Agency is about identity and\ncontrol, and about the capability to act on one’s goals or will [Epstein and Axtell, 1996, Gilbert, 2019, Barker\nand Jane, 2016]. Agents are endowed with decision-making capabilities, they sense changes in the environment,\ncommunicate, and act upon those changes [Wooldridge, 1999], see also Figure 2. Agents have been studied for\na long time, and occur in many fields of AI. From the definition of agents interacting with the environment,\ndifferent approaches focus on specific aspects of agents and agent behavior. In symbolic reasoning [Harman,\n1984] and game theory [Von Neumann and Morgenstern, 2007, Owen, 2013], the topic of study is decision making\nby rational agents. The field of multi-agent systems studies intelligent systems that emerge from the interaction\nwith different agents, human and/or artificial [Ferber and Weiss, 1999, Steels, 2003]. In machine learning, the\nfield of reinforcement learning studies how an agent can learn from interacting with an environment [Sutton\nand Barto, 2018]. In this context, agents are systems that would adapt their policy if their actions influenced\nthe world in a different way [Kenton et al., 2023]. In autonomous systems and robotics, agents act in order to\nachieve a goal [Liu and Wu, 2018]. Connectionism studies the emergence of intelligent behavior by embodied\nagents [Brooks, 1990, Medler, 1998]. Evolutionary algorithms [Yu and Gen, 2010, Bäck, 1996, Preuss, 2015] use\nnature-inspired agent-based computation in order to achieve robust and flexible optimization, of which the\nant-colony optimization algorithm [Dorigo et al., 2007] is a well known example.\n   For the purpose of this survey, we build upon the definitions from these traditions. We define agentic LLMs as:\n            Agents that receive input in natural language from their environment, reason to make decisions,\n            and take autonomous actions in affecting their environment, to achieve specific goals.\nWe stress that: agents may receive input and reason in natural or formal language; agents may plan to break\ndown complex goals into smaller steps; agents may reflect on their own actions; agents may use tools to retrieve\nnew information or to effect their actions; agent may build an internal model of the world; agents may have an\ninternal structure that consists of multiple agents; agents may assist humans in achieving their goals; agents may\ninteract in a society with humans and other agents; agents may create their own training data.\n   The categories reasoning–acting–interacting build upon each other: the technology that has been developed\nby the reasoning approaches (category 1) is used for increasingly intelligent acting by assistants. The interactive\nabilities of the assistants (category 2) enable social simulation experiments. The outcome of assistant actions\n(category 2) and of these social experiments (category 3) can be used for data augmentation (category 1), to\nfinetune LLMs (which can improve the accuracy of reasoning LLMs, etc.). This virtuous circle is depicted in\nFigure 1, and attracts interest from LLM researchers to agentic LLM methods [Sutskever, 2024, Guo et al., 2025, Du\n\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n", "path": "paper.pdf", "file_hash": "b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c", "kind": "pdf", "start_line": null, "end_line": null, "page": 4, "status": "valid", "seen_requests": ["request-0009", "request-0010", "request-0011"], "successful_requests": ["request-0009", "request-0010", "request-0011"], "step_id": 8, "sources": []}}
{"event": "code_execution", "time": 1793187704.4907842, "run_id": "c91ee58aa1e049578717e6c44905d773", "seq": 133, "step_id": 12, "stdout": "", "output_truncated": false, "error": {"code": "runtime_error", "message": "'evidence_id'"}, "tool_calls": [{"name": "update_plan", "args": {"items": [{"title": "检查工作副本、测试与报告初始结构", "status": "done"}, {"title": "分批读取 PDF 第1-52页并记录五主题笔记", "status": "doing"}, {"title": "核对主题问题、引文与页码", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "归并五份笔记并填写 report.json", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "运行 pytest 并完整验证报告", "status": "todo"}]}, "result": {"items": [{"title": "检查工作副本、测试与报告初始结构", "status": "done"}, {"title": "分批读取 PDF 第1-52页并记录五主题笔记", "status": "doing"}, {"title": "核对主题问题、引文与页码", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "归并五份笔记并填写 report.json", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "运行 pytest 并完整验证报告", "status": "todo"}]}}, {"name": "get_evidence", "args": {"evidence_id": "ev-000011"}, "result": {"id": "ev-000011", "content": "1:4   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\n\n\n\n\n                                      Fig. 2. LLM Agent as Assistant [Sypherd and Belle, 2024].\n\n\n\n   Agency is a central concept in artificial intelligence [Russell and Norvig, 2016]. Agency is about identity and\ncontrol, and about the capability to act on one’s goals or will [Epstein and Axtell, 1996, Gilbert, 2019, Barker\nand Jane, 2016]. Agents are endowed with decision-making capabilities, they sense changes in the environment,\ncommunicate, and act upon those changes [Wooldridge, 1999], see also Figure 2. Agents have been studied for\na long time, and occur in many fields of AI. From the definition of agents interacting with the environment,\ndifferent approaches focus on specific aspects of agents and agent behavior. In symbolic reasoning [Harman,\n1984] and game theory [Von Neumann and Morgenstern, 2007, Owen, 2013], the topic of study is decision making\nby rational agents. The field of multi-agent systems studies intelligent systems that emerge from the interaction\nwith different agents, human and/or artificial [Ferber and Weiss, 1999, Steels, 2003]. In machine learning, the\nfield of reinforcement learning studies how an agent can learn from interacting with an environment [Sutton\nand Barto, 2018]. In this context, agents are systems that would adapt their policy if their actions influenced\nthe world in a different way [Kenton et al., 2023]. In autonomous systems and robotics, agents act in order to\nachieve a goal [Liu and Wu, 2018]. Connectionism studies the emergence of intelligent behavior by embodied\nagents [Brooks, 1990, Medler, 1998]. Evolutionary algorithms [Yu and Gen, 2010, Bäck, 1996, Preuss, 2015] use\nnature-inspired agent-based computation in order to achieve robust and flexible optimization, of which the\nant-colony optimization algorithm [Dorigo et al., 2007] is a well known example.\n   For the purpose of this survey, we build upon the definitions from these traditions. We define agentic LLMs as:\n            Agents that receive input in natural language from their environment, reason to make decisions,\n            and take autonomous actions in affecting their environment, to achieve specific goals.\nWe stress that: agents may receive input and reason in natural or formal language; agents may plan to break\ndown complex goals into smaller steps; agents may reflect on their own actions; agents may use tools to retrieve\nnew information or to effect their actions; agent may build an internal model of the world; agents may have an\ninternal structure that consists of multiple agents; agents may assist humans in achieving their goals; agents may\ninteract in a society with humans and other agents; agents may create their own training data.\n   The categories reasoning–acting–interacting build upon each other: the technology that has been developed\nby the reasoning approaches (category 1) is used for increasingly intelligent acting by assistants. The interactive\nabilities of the assistants (category 2) enable social simulation experiments. The outcome of assistant actions\n(category 2) and of these social experiments (category 3) can be used for data augmentation (category 1), to\nfinetune LLMs (which can improve the accuracy of reasoning LLMs, etc.). This virtuous circle is depicted in\nFigure 1, and attracts interest from LLM researchers to agentic LLM methods [Sutskever, 2024, Guo et al., 2025, Du\n\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n", "path": "paper.pdf", "file_hash": "b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c", "kind": "pdf", "start_line": null, "end_line": null, "page": 4, "status": "valid", "seen_requests": ["request-0009", "request-0010", "request-0011"], "successful_requests": ["request-0009", "request-0010", "request-0011"], "step_id": 8, "sources": []}}], "namespace": [{"name": "eid", "type": "str", "size": 9, "stale": false}, {"name": "result", "type": "dict", "size": 13, "stale": false}], "sources": {"eid": [], "result": [{"path": "paper.pdf", "file_hash": "b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c"}]}}
