{"event": "code_execution", "time": 1793187858.6895485, "run_id": "c91ee58aa1e049578717e6c44905d773", "seq": 315, "step_id": 30, "stdout": "", "output_truncated": false, "error": null, "tool_calls": [{"name": "read_pdf", "args": {"start_page": 1, "end_page": 2}, "result": {"argv": ["/mnt/d/Muiti-Agent Programing/agents/code-agent/24371220/.venv/bin/python", "read_pdf.py", "--start-page", "1", "--end-page", "2"], "cwd": "/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/run-nh47wiy_/results/paper/repo", "rc": 0, "stdout": "{\"pdf\": \"/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/run-nh47wiy_/results/paper/repo/paper.pdf\", \"pages\": [{\"page\": 1, \"text\": \"                                         Agentic Large Language Models, a survey\\n                                         ASKE PLAAT∗ , Leiden University, Netherlands\\n                                         MAX VAN DUIJN, Leiden University, Netherlands\\n                                         NIKI VAN STEIN, Leiden University, Netherlands\\n                                         MIKE PREUSS, Leiden University, Netherlands\\n                                         PETER VAN DER PUTTEN, Leiden University & AI Lab, Pegasystems, Netherlands\\n                                         KEES JOOST BATENBURG, Leiden University, Netherlands\\n                                         Background: There is great interest in agentic LLMs, large language models that act as agents.\\narXiv:2503.23037v3 [cs.AI] 22 Nov 2025\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                                         Objectives: We review the growing body of work in this area and provide a research agenda.\\n                                         Methods: Agentic LLMs are LLMs that (1) reason, (2) act, and (3) interact. We organize the literature according to these three\\n                                         categories.\\n                                         Results: The research in the first category focuses on reasoning, reflection, and retrieval, aiming to improve decision making;\\n                                         the second category focuses on action models, robots, and tools, aiming for agents that act as useful assistants; the third\\n                                         category focuses on multi-agent systems, aiming for collaborative task solving and simulating interaction to study emergent\\n                                         social behavior. We find that works mutually benefit from results in other categories: retrieval enables tool use, reflection\\n                                         improves multi-agent collaboration, and reasoning benefits all categories.\\n                                         Conclusions: We discuss applications of agentic LLMs and provide an agenda for further research. Important applications are\\n                                         in medical diagnosis, logistics and financial market analysis. Meanwhile, self-reflective agents playing roles and interacting\\n                                         with one another augment the process of scientific research itself. Further, agentic LLMs provide a solution for the problem of\\n                                         LLMs running out of training data: inference-time behavior generates new training states, such that LLMs can keep learning\\n                                         without needing ever larger datasets. We note that there is risk associated with LLM assistants taking action in the real\\n                                         world—safety, liability and security are open problems—while agentic LLMs are also likely to benefit society.\\n                                         JAIR Associate Editor: Kai-Wei Chang\\n                                         JAIR Reference Format:\\n                                         Aske Plaat, Max van Duijn, Niki van Stein, Mike Preuss, Peter van der Putten, and Kees Joost Batenburg. 2025. Agentic\\n                                         Large Language Models, a survey. Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research 1, Article 1 (December 2025), 76 pages. doi:\\n                                         10.1613/jair.1.xxxxx\\n\\n                                                                                                       Contents\\n                                            Abstract                                                                                                                                1\\n                                            Contents                                                                                                                                1\\n                                            1    Introduction                                                                                                                       2\\n                                            1.1      Agentic LLMs: Reasoning–Acting–Interacting                                                                                     3\\n                                         ∗ Corresponding Author.\\n\\n\\n                                         Authors’ Contact Information: Aske Plaat, orcid: 0000-0001-7202-3322, aske.plaat@gmail.com, Leiden University, Leiden, Netherlands; Max\\n                                         van Duijn, Leiden University, Leiden, Netherlands; Niki van Stein, Leiden University, Leiden, Netherlands; Mike Preuss, Leiden University,\\n                                         Leiden, Netherlands; Peter van der Putten, Leiden University & AI Lab, Pegasystems, Leiden, Netherlands; Kees Joost Batenburg, Leiden\\n                                         University, Leiden, Netherlands.\\n\\n\\n                                                                    This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution International 4.0 License.\\n\\n\\n                                         © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).\\n                                         doi: 10.1613/jair.1.xxxxx\\n\\n\\n                                                                                      Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}, {\"page\": 2, \"text\": \"1:2    •    Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\\n\\n\\n      1.2        Literature Selection                                                                                   5\\n      1.3        LLM Training Pipeline                                                                                  5\\n      1.4        The Need for Agentic LLMs                                                                              6\\n      1.5        Taxonomy                                                                                               7\\n      2       Reasoning                                                                                                 8\\n      2.1        Multi Step Reasoning                                                                                   9\\n      2.2        Self Reflection                                                                                       13\\n      2.3        Retrieval Augmentation                                                                                17\\n      2.4        Discussion                                                                                            17\\n      3       Acting                                                                                                   20\\n      3.1        Action Models                                                                                         20\\n      3.2        Robots and Tools                                                                                      21\\n      3.3        Assistants                                                                                            25\\n      3.4        Discussion                                                                                            30\\n      4       Interacting                                                                                              31\\n      4.1        Social Capabilities of LLMs                                                                           32\\n      4.2        Role-Based Interaction                                                                                35\\n      4.3        Simulating Open-ended Societies                                                                       39\\n      4.4        Discussion                                                                                            45\\n      5       General Discussion and Research Agenda                                                                   48\\n      5.1        Research Agenda for Agentic LLM                                                                       48\\n      5.2        Conclusion                                                                                            52\\n      6       Reproducibility Checklist for JAIR                                                                       75\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n1      Introduction\\nThe strength of the language abilities of LLMs has taken the world by storm. Recent work has extended their\\nabilities with reasoning, information retrieval, and interaction tools. As a result, LLMs are now increasingly able\\nto act as agents in the world [Shen, 2024, Qin et al., 2023]. This ability has increased the relevance of LLMs to\\nsociety and science. Agentic LLMs are being used to assist in medicine, logistics, finance, and other application\\nareas. Their ability to self-reflect, interact, and play roles enables new types of research, including large-scale\\nsocial science simulations. We survey the growing body of literature on agentic LLMs, which we define as large\\nlanguage models that (1) reason, (2) act, and (3) interact. We organize this article accordingly.\\n   Agentic LLMs are also relevant in the acquisition of new training data for artificial intelligence (AI). Traditionally,\\nLLMs have been trained on large datasets. Recently, however, it is getting harder to scale and improve datasets\\nfurther, and training performance is reportedly plateauing, at high energy cost [Sutskever, 2024]. By interacting\\nwith the world, agents generate new empirical data (see Figure 1). This data can be used for additional training\\n(pretraining or finetuning) or to enhance performance at inference time, provided there is adequate grounding\\nthrough human or automated validation and filtering [Subramaniam et al., 2025]. An example of how LLMs can\\nbe trained based on their own actions, are Vision-Language-Action models, that update weights according to\\nrobotic action-feedback sequences [Black et al., 2024, Chiang et al., 2024, Yang et al., 2025b]. Thus, in addition to\\n\\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}]}\n", "stderr": "", "timed_out": false, "pdf": "/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/run-nh47wiy_/results/paper/repo/paper.pdf", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "                                         Agentic Large Language Models, a survey\n                                         ASKE PLAAT∗ , Leiden University, Netherlands\n                                         MAX VAN DUIJN, Leiden University, Netherlands\n                                         NIKI VAN STEIN, Leiden University, Netherlands\n                                         MIKE PREUSS, Leiden University, Netherlands\n                                         PETER VAN DER PUTTEN, Leiden University & AI Lab, Pegasystems, Netherlands\n                                         KEES JOOST BATENBURG, Leiden University, Netherlands\n                                         Background: There is great interest in agentic LLMs, large language models that act as agents.\narXiv:2503.23037v3 [cs.AI] 22 Nov 2025\n\n\n\n\n                                         Objectives: We review the growing body of work in this area and provide a research agenda.\n                                         Methods: Agentic LLMs are LLMs that (1) reason, (2) act, and (3) interact. We organize the literature according to these three\n                                         categories.\n                                         Results: The research in the first category focuses on reasoning, reflection, and retrieval, aiming to improve decision making;\n                                         the second category focuses on action models, robots, and tools, aiming for agents that act as useful assistants; the third\n                                         category focuses on multi-agent systems, aiming for collaborative task solving and simulating interaction to study emergent\n                                         social behavior. We find that works mutually benefit from results in other categories: retrieval enables tool use, reflection\n                                         improves multi-agent collaboration, and reasoning benefits all categories.\n                                         Conclusions: We discuss applications of agentic LLMs and provide an agenda for further research. Important applications are\n                                         in medical diagnosis, logistics and financial market analysis. Meanwhile, self-reflective agents playing roles and interacting\n                                         with one another augment the process of scientific research itself. Further, agentic LLMs provide a solution for the problem of\n                                         LLMs running out of training data: inference-time behavior generates new training states, such that LLMs can keep learning\n                                         without needing ever larger datasets. We note that there is risk associated with LLM assistants taking action in the real\n                                         world—safety, liability and security are open problems—while agentic LLMs are also likely to benefit society.\n                                         JAIR Associate Editor: Kai-Wei Chang\n                                         JAIR Reference Format:\n                                         Aske Plaat, Max van Duijn, Niki van Stein, Mike Preuss, Peter van der Putten, and Kees Joost Batenburg. 2025. Agentic\n                                         Large Language Models, a survey. Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research 1, Article 1 (December 2025), 76 pages. doi:\n                                         10.1613/jair.1.xxxxx\n\n                                                                                                       Contents\n                                            Abstract                                                                                                                                1\n                                            Contents                                                                                                                                1\n                                            1    Introduction                                                                                                                       2\n                                            1.1      Agentic LLMs: Reasoning–Acting–Interacting                                                                                     3\n                                         ∗ Corresponding Author.\n\n\n                                         Authors’ Contact Information: Aske Plaat, orcid: 0000-0001-7202-3322, aske.plaat@gmail.com, Leiden University, Leiden, Netherlands; Max\n                                         van Duijn, Leiden University, Leiden, Netherlands; Niki van Stein, Leiden University, Leiden, Netherlands; Mike Preuss, Leiden University,\n                                         Leiden, Netherlands; Peter van der Putten, Leiden University & AI Lab, Pegasystems, Leiden, Netherlands; Kees Joost Batenburg, Leiden\n                                         University, Leiden, Netherlands.\n\n\n                                                                    This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution International 4.0 License.\n\n\n                                         © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).\n                                         doi: 10.1613/jair.1.xxxxx\n\n\n                                                                                      Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}, {"page": 2, "text": "1:2    •    Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\n\n\n      1.2        Literature Selection                                                                                   5\n      1.3        LLM Training Pipeline                                                                                  5\n      1.4        The Need for Agentic LLMs                                                                              6\n      1.5        Taxonomy                                                                                               7\n      2       Reasoning                                                                                                 8\n      2.1        Multi Step Reasoning                                                                                   9\n      2.2        Self Reflection                                                                                       13\n      2.3        Retrieval Augmentation                                                                                17\n      2.4        Discussion                                                                                            17\n      3       Acting                                                                                                   20\n      3.1        Action Models                                                                                         20\n      3.2        Robots and Tools                                                                                      21\n      3.3        Assistants                                                                                            25\n      3.4        Discussion                                                                                            30\n      4       Interacting                                                                                              31\n      4.1        Social Capabilities of LLMs                                                                           32\n      4.2        Role-Based Interaction                                                                                35\n      4.3        Simulating Open-ended Societies                                                                       39\n      4.4        Discussion                                                                                            45\n      5       General Discussion and Research Agenda                                                                   48\n      5.1        Research Agenda for Agentic LLM                                                                       48\n      5.2        Conclusion                                                                                            52\n      6       Reproducibility Checklist for JAIR                                                                       75\n\n\n\n\n1      Introduction\nThe strength of the language abilities of LLMs has taken the world by storm. Recent work has extended their\nabilities with reasoning, information retrieval, and interaction tools. As a result, LLMs are now increasingly able\nto act as agents in the world [Shen, 2024, Qin et al., 2023]. This ability has increased the relevance of LLMs to\nsociety and science. Agentic LLMs are being used to assist in medicine, logistics, finance, and other application\nareas. Their ability to self-reflect, interact, and play roles enables new types of research, including large-scale\nsocial science simulations. We survey the growing body of literature on agentic LLMs, which we define as large\nlanguage models that (1) reason, (2) act, and (3) interact. We organize this article accordingly.\n   Agentic LLMs are also relevant in the acquisition of new training data for artificial intelligence (AI). Traditionally,\nLLMs have been trained on large datasets. Recently, however, it is getting harder to scale and improve datasets\nfurther, and training performance is reportedly plateauing, at high energy cost [Sutskever, 2024]. By interacting\nwith the world, agents generate new empirical data (see Figure 1). This data can be used for additional training\n(pretraining or finetuning) or to enhance performance at inference time, provided there is adequate grounding\nthrough human or automated validation and filtering [Subramaniam et al., 2025]. An example of how LLMs can\nbe trained based on their own actions, are Vision-Language-Action models, that update weights according to\nrobotic action-feedback sequences [Black et al., 2024, Chiang et al., 2024, Yang et al., 2025b]. Thus, in addition to\n\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}], "evidence_ids": ["ev-000069", "ev-000070"]}}, {"name": "read_pdf", "args": {"start_page": 45, "end_page": 46}, "result": {"argv": ["/mnt/d/Muiti-Agent Programing/agents/code-agent/24371220/.venv/bin/python", "read_pdf.py", "--start-page", "45", "--end-page", "46"], "cwd": "/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/run-nh47wiy_/results/paper/repo", "rc": 0, "stdout": "{\"pdf\": \"/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/run-nh47wiy_/results/paper/repo/paper.pdf\", \"pages\": [{\"page\": 45, \"text\": \"                                                                                                              Agentic LLM     •   1:45\\n\\n\\n    Games are eminently suited as open-ended benchmarks for interactive behavior. Real-world tasks require\\nhandling intricate interactions, advanced spatial reasoning, long-term planning, and continuous exploration of\\nnew strategies. Balrog [Paglieri et al., 2024] incorporates reinforcement learning environments of varying levels\\nof difficulty, ranging from tasks that are solvable by non-expert humans in seconds to challenging ones that may\\ntake years to master (such as the NetHack Learning Environment). They find that while current models achieve\\npartial success in the easier games, they struggle significantly with more challenging tasks such as vision-based\\ndecision-making.\\n    Progress in machine learning depends on benchmark availability. As models evolve, there is a need to create\\nbenchmarks that can measure progress on new generative capabilities [Butt et al., 2024]. BenchAgents decomposes\\nthe benchmark creation process into planning, generation, data verification, and evaluation, each of which is\\nexecuted by an LLM agent. These agents interact with each other and utilize human-in-the-loop feedback to\\nexplicitly improve and flexibly control data diversity and quality. BenchAgents creates benchmarks to evaluate\\ncapabilities related to planning and constraint satisfaction.\\n    AgentBoard provides an evaluation of the breadth of existing benchmarks [Ma et al., 2024a]. Benchmarks\\nshould have task diversity. It is necessary to cover various agent tasks such as embodied, web, and tool tasks.\\nAdditionally, multi round interaction is important, to mimic realistic scenarios. Existing benchmarks typically\\nadopt single-round tasks. Furthermore, agents should be evaluated in partially-observable environments, to test\\nif they can actively explore their surroundings. Existing agent benchmarks fail to satisfy all of these criteria [Ma\\net al., 2024a].\\n\\n\\n4.4   Discussion\\nIn this third part of the taxonomy, the focus was on agents that interact with other agents, both human and\\nartificial. The goal is to understand social interaction, from interaction in conversations, social scenarios and\\ndilemmas, to role-playing in duos and small teams, to large-scale open-ended emergent behavior at society level.\\n\\n4.4.1 Interaction Studies. Over the past years, LLMs have provided us with new instances of human-machine\\ninteraction. Users across the globe have engaged in chat conversations seeking assistance with tasks in their\\nprofessional or private lives. To engage in such interactions, LLMs rely on functions learned during training that\\nwe can recognize as social, including abilities for conversation, politeness and etiquette, handling of emotional\\nand affective states, strategizing, and theory of mind. Such abilities form the basis not only for human-machine\\ninteraction, but also for LLM-driven machine-machine interactions, as we discuss next.\\n   When interacting in multi-agent environments, agentic LLMs show varying levels of performance on games\\nwith strategic and coordinated behavior. Enhancing models with reasoning capacities boosts performance, as is\\nevidenced by GPT-o1’s better overall performance and the positive effect of adding probabilistic graphs [Xu et al.,\\n2024a]. Pre-defined roles and interaction protocols (cooperative or adversarial) help structure the communication\\nbetween LLM agents while improving task performance. Role-playing frameworks, AI feedback loops, and debate\\nmoderation suggests that carefully coordinating multiple LLMs can harness their collective intelligence and yield\\noutcomes that surpass single model performance.\\n   We have covered open-ended multi-LLM simulations without prior role assignment. These simulations give\\na new impulse to long-standing interests in the social sciences to model self-organizing behaviors, collective\\nintelligence and the development of social conventions and norms. The scale of such simulations varies from a\\nfew interacting agents up to a million. Emergent behaviors are observed, such as coordination through norms\\nand social structures that form spontaneously. In open-world approaches LLMs are used to create increasingly\\ncomplex challenges and solutions for LLM agents.\\n\\n                                      Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}, {\"page\": 46, \"text\": \"1:46   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\\n\\n\\n4.4.2 In Depth: CAMEL and Generative Agents. We have surveyed the individual methods for social interaction.\\nIn order to dig deeper and highlight some of the issues in this area, we will now discuss two approaches, CAMEL,\\nand Generative Agents, in more detail.\\n   CAMEL. In Section 4.2.2 we saw how LLMs can be prompted to perform different roles, and work together\\nin solving tasks. CAMEL [Li et al., 2023a] is a multi-agent system that has been designed to perform this role-\\nplaying-for-problem-solving task. Just like the assistants in Section 3.3, the goal is to solve a task, for example in\\nmedicine, finance, or computer programming (see also Figure 22). One approach is to write a single monolithic\\nprompt for the LLM in which all the instructions to solve the task are specified—or so the author of the prompt\\nhopes. To measure the success of this approach, a benchmark of test-cases can be created that the LLM has to\\nsolve.\\n   The approach of CAMEL is different. CAMEL starts with the idea that specialized agents, with different prompts,\\nmay work better. Furthermore, the idea of CAMEL is that the different agents may work better together, and\\nthat new problem solving approaches may emerge, that were not present in the single monolithic prompt, or\\nin the initial prompts of the individual agents. CAMEL is a multi-agent system that consists of two agents, an\\nassistant and a user. The user has the domain knowledge, can provide a task specification, as well as feedback on\\nintermediate deliverables that the assistant makes. The assistant has certain skills, for example Python coding, to\\nwrite a program to solve the problem that the user agent specifies. Both agents are given a so-called inception\\nprompt, an initial idea. They then further send messages (prompts) to each other as they work towards solving\\nthe task.\\n   The CAMEL paper describes an experiment where a powerful LLM, GPT4, generates the initial prompt, and a\\ncheaper LLM, GPT-3.5-turbo, executes the further steps. Ten different tasks have been simulated by a combination\\nof 50 agents and 50 users, for a total of 25000 conversations (that were depth-limited to 40 messages).\\n   In such a setup where agents work with agents, the training process may become unstable. CAMEL reports\\nproblems where the assistant simply repeats instructions (instead of answering them), problems with fake replies,\\nempty replies, infinite loops of messages, and role reversal. A special (third) critic agent was introduced to ensure\\na constructive communication process between the agents.\\n   The final experiments report success. The multi-agent system performed better than a single prompt, with\\nperformance on the HumanEval benchmark improving form 30% to around 50%. The CAMEL experiments showed\\nthat a multi-agent LLM system can be used for problem solving, and the authors showed how stable learning can\\nbe achieved when attention is paid to a constructive communication process.\\n   Generative Agents. The architecture of CAMEL recognized two types of agents, assistant and user. The next\\ntwo papers that we discuss, Park et al. [2023, 2024], are about simulating a society with a larger number of agents,\\nfrom 10-1000. The first paper [Park et al., 2023] introduces the generative agents’ architecture, an architecture\\nthat was designed with the aim of generating believable proxies of human behavior. The architecture consists of\\na memory, a planning module, and a reflection module. In this design agents are given roles and operate in a\\nSims-like environment. An example is described where they decide to organize a Valentine’s day party (see also\\nFigure 27, and the live web simulation).5 The focus of this work is on achieving believable social behavior, that is\\nunscripted: behavior that emerges from the communications by the agents. We quote the paper: agents wake up,\\ncook breakfast, and head to work; artists paint, while authors write; they form opinions, notice each other, and initiate\\nconversations; they remember and reflect on days past as they plan the next day. The number of agents is larger\\nthan in CAMEL, arond 5-15, and the agent architecture is also more involved. Where in CAMEL the behavior was\\nspecified fully in-context, in the prompts that are exchanged, in Generative Agents there are additional external\\nalgorithms for memory, planning, and reflection. The paper further notes that: the new goals require architectures\\n5 See https://reverie.herokuapp.com/arXiv_Demo/\\n\\n\\n\\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}]}\n", "stderr": "", "timed_out": false, "pdf": "/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/run-nh47wiy_/results/paper/repo/paper.pdf", "pages": [{"page": 45, "text": "                                                                                                              Agentic LLM     •   1:45\n\n\n    Games are eminently suited as open-ended benchmarks for interactive behavior. Real-world tasks require\nhandling intricate interactions, advanced spatial reasoning, long-term planning, and continuous exploration of\nnew strategies. Balrog [Paglieri et al., 2024] incorporates reinforcement learning environments of varying levels\nof difficulty, ranging from tasks that are solvable by non-expert humans in seconds to challenging ones that may\ntake years to master (such as the NetHack Learning Environment). They find that while current models achieve\npartial success in the easier games, they struggle significantly with more challenging tasks such as vision-based\ndecision-making.\n    Progress in machine learning depends on benchmark availability. As models evolve, there is a need to create\nbenchmarks that can measure progress on new generative capabilities [Butt et al., 2024]. BenchAgents decomposes\nthe benchmark creation process into planning, generation, data verification, and evaluation, each of which is\nexecuted by an LLM agent. These agents interact with each other and utilize human-in-the-loop feedback to\nexplicitly improve and flexibly control data diversity and quality. BenchAgents creates benchmarks to evaluate\ncapabilities related to planning and constraint satisfaction.\n    AgentBoard provides an evaluation of the breadth of existing benchmarks [Ma et al., 2024a]. Benchmarks\nshould have task diversity. It is necessary to cover various agent tasks such as embodied, web, and tool tasks.\nAdditionally, multi round interaction is important, to mimic realistic scenarios. Existing benchmarks typically\nadopt single-round tasks. Furthermore, agents should be evaluated in partially-observable environments, to test\nif they can actively explore their surroundings. Existing agent benchmarks fail to satisfy all of these criteria [Ma\net al., 2024a].\n\n\n4.4   Discussion\nIn this third part of the taxonomy, the focus was on agents that interact with other agents, both human and\nartificial. The goal is to understand social interaction, from interaction in conversations, social scenarios and\ndilemmas, to role-playing in duos and small teams, to large-scale open-ended emergent behavior at society level.\n\n4.4.1 Interaction Studies. Over the past years, LLMs have provided us with new instances of human-machine\ninteraction. Users across the globe have engaged in chat conversations seeking assistance with tasks in their\nprofessional or private lives. To engage in such interactions, LLMs rely on functions learned during training that\nwe can recognize as social, including abilities for conversation, politeness and etiquette, handling of emotional\nand affective states, strategizing, and theory of mind. Such abilities form the basis not only for human-machine\ninteraction, but also for LLM-driven machine-machine interactions, as we discuss next.\n   When interacting in multi-agent environments, agentic LLMs show varying levels of performance on games\nwith strategic and coordinated behavior. Enhancing models with reasoning capacities boosts performance, as is\nevidenced by GPT-o1’s better overall performance and the positive effect of adding probabilistic graphs [Xu et al.,\n2024a]. Pre-defined roles and interaction protocols (cooperative or adversarial) help structure the communication\nbetween LLM agents while improving task performance. Role-playing frameworks, AI feedback loops, and debate\nmoderation suggests that carefully coordinating multiple LLMs can harness their collective intelligence and yield\noutcomes that surpass single model performance.\n   We have covered open-ended multi-LLM simulations without prior role assignment. These simulations give\na new impulse to long-standing interests in the social sciences to model self-organizing behaviors, collective\nintelligence and the development of social conventions and norms. The scale of such simulations varies from a\nfew interacting agents up to a million. Emergent behaviors are observed, such as coordination through norms\nand social structures that form spontaneously. In open-world approaches LLMs are used to create increasingly\ncomplex challenges and solutions for LLM agents.\n\n                                      Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}, {"page": 46, "text": "1:46   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\n\n\n4.4.2 In Depth: CAMEL and Generative Agents. We have surveyed the individual methods for social interaction.\nIn order to dig deeper and highlight some of the issues in this area, we will now discuss two approaches, CAMEL,\nand Generative Agents, in more detail.\n   CAMEL. In Section 4.2.2 we saw how LLMs can be prompted to perform different roles, and work together\nin solving tasks. CAMEL [Li et al., 2023a] is a multi-agent system that has been designed to perform this role-\nplaying-for-problem-solving task. Just like the assistants in Section 3.3, the goal is to solve a task, for example in\nmedicine, finance, or computer programming (see also Figure 22). One approach is to write a single monolithic\nprompt for the LLM in which all the instructions to solve the task are specified—or so the author of the prompt\nhopes. To measure the success of this approach, a benchmark of test-cases can be created that the LLM has to\nsolve.\n   The approach of CAMEL is different. CAMEL starts with the idea that specialized agents, with different prompts,\nmay work better. Furthermore, the idea of CAMEL is that the different agents may work better together, and\nthat new problem solving approaches may emerge, that were not present in the single monolithic prompt, or\nin the initial prompts of the individual agents. CAMEL is a multi-agent system that consists of two agents, an\nassistant and a user. The user has the domain knowledge, can provide a task specification, as well as feedback on\nintermediate deliverables that the assistant makes. The assistant has certain skills, for example Python coding, to\nwrite a program to solve the problem that the user agent specifies. Both agents are given a so-called inception\nprompt, an initial idea. They then further send messages (prompts) to each other as they work towards solving\nthe task.\n   The CAMEL paper describes an experiment where a powerful LLM, GPT4, generates the initial prompt, and a\ncheaper LLM, GPT-3.5-turbo, executes the further steps. Ten different tasks have been simulated by a combination\nof 50 agents and 50 users, for a total of 25000 conversations (that were depth-limited to 40 messages).\n   In such a setup where agents work with agents, the training process may become unstable. CAMEL reports\nproblems where the assistant simply repeats instructions (instead of answering them), problems with fake replies,\nempty replies, infinite loops of messages, and role reversal. A special (third) critic agent was introduced to ensure\na constructive communication process between the agents.\n   The final experiments report success. The multi-agent system performed better than a single prompt, with\nperformance on the HumanEval benchmark improving form 30% to around 50%. The CAMEL experiments showed\nthat a multi-agent LLM system can be used for problem solving, and the authors showed how stable learning can\nbe achieved when attention is paid to a constructive communication process.\n   Generative Agents. The architecture of CAMEL recognized two types of agents, assistant and user. The next\ntwo papers that we discuss, Park et al. [2023, 2024], are about simulating a society with a larger number of agents,\nfrom 10-1000. The first paper [Park et al., 2023] introduces the generative agents’ architecture, an architecture\nthat was designed with the aim of generating believable proxies of human behavior. The architecture consists of\na memory, a planning module, and a reflection module. In this design agents are given roles and operate in a\nSims-like environment. An example is described where they decide to organize a Valentine’s day party (see also\nFigure 27, and the live web simulation).5 The focus of this work is on achieving believable social behavior, that is\nunscripted: behavior that emerges from the communications by the agents. We quote the paper: agents wake up,\ncook breakfast, and head to work; artists paint, while authors write; they form opinions, notice each other, and initiate\nconversations; they remember and reflect on days past as they plan the next day. The number of agents is larger\nthan in CAMEL, arond 5-15, and the agent architecture is also more involved. Where in CAMEL the behavior was\nspecified fully in-context, in the prompts that are exchanged, in Generative Agents there are additional external\nalgorithms for memory, planning, and reflection. The paper further notes that: the new goals require architectures\n5 See https://reverie.herokuapp.com/arXiv_Demo/\n\n\n\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}], "evidence_ids": ["ev-000071", "ev-000072"]}}, {"name": "update_plan", "args": {"items": [{"title": "检查工作副本、测试与报告初始结构", "status": "done"}, {"title": "分批读取 PDF 第1-52页并记录五主题笔记", "status": "doing"}, {"title": "核对主题问题、引文与页码", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "归并五份笔记并填写 report.json", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "运行 pytest 并完整验证报告", "status": "todo"}]}, "result": {"items": [{"title": "检查工作副本、测试与报告初始结构", "status": "done"}, {"title": "分批读取 PDF 第1-52页并记录五主题笔记", "status": "doing"}, {"title": "核对主题问题、引文与页码", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "归并五份笔记并填写 report.json", "status": "todo"}, {"title": "运行 pytest 并完整验证报告", "status": "todo"}]}}], "namespace": [{"name": "eid", "type": "str", "size": 9, "stale": false}, {"name": "pdf_batch", "type": "dict", "size": 7, "stale": false}, {"name": "plan", "type": "dict", "size": 1, "stale": false}, {"name": "r", "type": "dict", "size": 7, "stale": false}, {"name": "r1", "type": "dict", "size": 9, "stale": false}, {"name": "r2", "type": "dict", "size": 9, "stale": false}, {"name": "r3", "type": "dict", "size": 13, "stale": false}, {"name": "result", "type": "dict", "size": 1, "stale": false}], "sources": {"eid": [], "result": [], "r1": [], "r2": [